window.MLP_QBANK=[{"id": "fundamentals-90ea0ca", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain the bias–variance trade-off.", "a": "<p>Expected test error = bias² + variance + irreducible noise. Bias is error from overly simple assumptions (underfitting); variance is error from sensitivity to the specific training sample (overfitting). Increasing model complexity typically lowers bias and raises variance; the goal is the complexity that minimises total error, found via validation. Regularisation, more data and ensembling shift the trade-off.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-1155af6", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "How do you detect and prevent overfitting?", "a": "<p>Detect: training performance much better than validation/CV performance; learning curves with a persistent gap. Prevent: more data or augmentation, simpler model, regularisation (L1/L2, dropout), early stopping, feature selection, ensembling (bagging), proper CV for tuning, and removing leakage.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-91bf57e", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?", "a": "<p>Supervised learning trains on labelled examples to predict a target (classification/regression) and is evaluated against true labels. Unsupervised learning has no labels and finds structure — clusters, low-dimensional representations, density/anomalies — and evaluation is harder (internal metrics, downstream usefulness).</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-e9ef60e", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Parametric vs non-parametric models?", "a": "<p>Parametric models have a fixed number of parameters and strong functional assumptions (linear regression, logistic regression, Naive Bayes) — efficient, need less data, may underfit. Non-parametric models grow in complexity with data (kNN, trees, kernel methods, GPs) — flexible, need more data, risk overfitting, often slower at prediction.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-e8735e3", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Generative vs discriminative models?", "a": "<p>Generative models learn P(x|y) and P(y) (hence the joint) and use Bayes' rule to classify; they can generate samples and handle missing data (Naive Bayes, GMM, HMM). Discriminative models learn P(y|x) or the boundary directly (logistic regression, SVM, trees). Discriminative models usually have lower asymptotic error; generative ones can be better with little data.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-c235d81", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we need a separate validation and test set?", "a": "<p>The validation set (or CV) is used repeatedly for model selection and hyperparameter tuning, so performance on it is optimistically biased. The test set is touched once at the end to give an unbiased estimate of generalisation. Reusing the test set for decisions turns it into another validation set.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-4463765", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the curse of dimensionality?", "a": "<p>As features grow, volume grows exponentially, so data become sparse, distances between points concentrate (nearest ≈ farthest), and the amount of data needed to generalise grows exponentially. Distance-based methods suffer most. Remedies: feature selection, dimensionality reduction, regularisation, domain knowledge.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-5d54fd4", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the difference between a loss function and an evaluation metric?", "a": "<p>The loss is optimised during training and needs mathematical convenience (differentiable, convex where possible) — e.g. log-loss. The metric measures what the business cares about and may be non-differentiable — e.g. F1, precision@k, revenue. Choose a loss aligned with the metric and select thresholds/hyperparameters on the metric.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-f7b2825", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Batch vs stochastic vs mini-batch gradient descent?", "a": "<p>Batch uses the full dataset per update: accurate gradients, expensive, smooth convergence. Stochastic uses one sample: cheap and noisy, can escape shallow local minima, needs decaying learning rates. Mini-batch (32–1024) balances both and exploits vectorised hardware — the default in practice.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-d79113c", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is the relationship between MLE, MAP and regularisation?", "a": "<p>MLE maximises the likelihood. MAP maximises likelihood × prior; taking logs adds −log prior to the loss. A zero-mean Gaussian prior adds an L2 penalty (Ridge/weight decay); a Laplace prior adds an L1 penalty (Lasso). So regularisation = encoding a prior belief that parameters are small.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-9257f25", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What would you do if your model performs well on training data but poorly in production?", "a": "<p>Check for data leakage in training features; train/serve skew (different preprocessing or feature computation online); distribution shift (data/concept drift) between training period and now; a validation scheme that didn't mirror production (random split vs time split, unseen groups); label definition differences; and bugs in the serving pipeline. Then fix the root cause and set up monitoring.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-6ef435d", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you approach a new ML problem?", "a": "<p>Clarify the business goal and how predictions will be used; define target, metric and baseline; check data availability, quality and leakage; choose a validation scheme mirroring production; build a simple baseline; iterate on features and models with error analysis; evaluate on slices; consider deployment constraints and monitoring; communicate trade-offs.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-d16bc92", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the no free lunch theorem and what does it mean in practice?", "a": "<p>Averaged over all possible data-generating distributions, every learning algorithm performs equally. A model is only better when its inductive bias matches the problem. Practically: no universally best model — try several reasonable ones and validate on your data.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-2bc1a48", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "Can a model have both high bias and high variance?", "a": "<p>Yes — e.g. a model with the wrong functional form (bias) that's also fit on tiny noisy data with many parameters (variance), or a deep tree on badly chosen features. The decomposition terms aren't strictly opposed; good features can reduce both.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-7e8ea74", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is inductive bias?", "a": "<p>The set of assumptions a learning algorithm uses to generalise beyond seen data: linearity (linear models), smoothness/locality (kNN, RBF), axis-aligned piecewise-constant structure (trees), translation equivariance (CNNs), feature independence (Naive Bayes). Choosing a model is choosing an inductive bias.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-f6d1b01", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "How do you handle missing values?", "a": "<p>First understand why they're missing (MCAR/MAR/MNAR) and how much. Options: drop rows/columns if few/mostly missing; simple imputation (median/mode) plus a missing-indicator flag; model-based (KNN, iterative/MICE); time-series fill (forward-fill/interpolate); or models that handle NaN natively (gradient boosting). Fit imputers on training data only, and validate the choice with CV.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-6e19781", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Explain MCAR, MAR and MNAR.", "a": "<p>MCAR: missingness is unrelated to any data — deleting is unbiased. MAR: missingness depends on observed variables — imputation conditioning on them works. MNAR: missingness depends on the unobserved value itself (e.g. high incomes withheld) — any method is biased without modelling the mechanism; missing indicators help capture it.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-a1a0a79", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "When should you normalise vs standardise?", "a": "<p>Standardise (z-score) as the general default for linear models, SVM, PCA, kNN, neural nets — it centres and handles unbounded data. Min-max normalise when you need a bounded range (e.g. pixel intensities, some NN inputs) and there are no strong outliers. RobustScaler with outliers. Trees need neither.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-d78cf63", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "One-hot vs label encoding — when to use each?", "a": "<p>One-hot for nominal categories in linear/distance-based models (no false order). Label/ordinal encoding for ordinal categories, or for tree models where arbitrary integer codes are acceptable. For high cardinality, use target/frequency/hashing encoding or native categorical support.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-8695a47", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is target encoding and its risk?", "a": "<p>Replacing each category with the (smoothed) mean of the target for that category. Risk: target leakage — a row's own label influences its feature, and rare categories get extreme values that memorise labels. Mitigate with out-of-fold (cross-fitted) encoding, smoothing toward the global mean, and adding noise.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-7a02ed6", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is data leakage? Give examples.", "a": "<p>When training uses information that won't be available at prediction time, inflating offline metrics. Examples: features recorded after the outcome (target leakage), fitting scalers/imputers/feature selection/target encoders on all data before splitting, random splits on time series, the same customer in train and test, duplicates across splits.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-9cd9df7", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you deal with an imbalanced dataset?", "a": "<p>Choose suitable metrics (PR-AUC, F1, recall@precision, cost); stratify splits; apply class weights or resampling (undersampling, SMOTE) only on training folds; tune the decision threshold on validation; consider ensemble methods like balanced random forests; get more minority data; recalibrate probabilities if needed.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-71f2605", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How does SMOTE work and what are its drawbacks?", "a": "<p>For each minority sample, pick one of its k nearest minority neighbours and create a synthetic point at a random position on the line between them. Drawbacks: can create noisy/overlapping samples in class-overlap regions, ignores the majority class distribution, struggles in high dimensions and with categorical features (use SMOTENC), and must be applied only inside training folds. Often class weights + threshold tuning do as well.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-c66c6c8", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Filter vs wrapper vs embedded feature selection?", "a": "<p>Filter: rank features by statistics independent of the model (correlation, chi², mutual information) — fast but ignores interactions. Wrapper: search subsets by training models (RFE, forward/backward) — accounts for interactions, expensive, overfit-prone. Embedded: selection during training (Lasso, tree importances) — efficient compromise.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-60dc24f", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you detect and treat outliers?", "a": "<p>Detect with visualisation, z-scores, IQR rule, robust z (MAD), or multivariate methods (Isolation Forest, Mahalanobis). Investigate cause: data error (fix/remove), legitimate extreme (keep, maybe transform/cap), or the signal itself (fraud). Treatments: winsorising, log transforms, robust scaling, robust losses, tree models.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-5b8c62c", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How would you encode a feature with 10,000 categories?", "a": "<p>Target encoding with cross-fitting and smoothing, frequency encoding, hashing trick, grouping rare categories into “Other”, learned embeddings (neural nets), or native categorical support in LightGBM/CatBoost. Avoid one-hot (sparse and huge) unless using a linear model on sparse matrices.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-6cbb766", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why should preprocessing be inside a pipeline?", "a": "<p>So every transformation is fit only on training folds during CV and applied identically at inference — preventing leakage and train/serve skew — and so the whole thing can be tuned (GridSearch over preprocessing params) and serialised as one artifact.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-b0aeebd", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "How would you encode cyclical features like hour of day?", "a": "<p>Map to the unit circle: sin(2π·hour/24) and cos(2π·hour/24), so 23:00 and 00:00 are close. Trees can also handle raw hour with splits, and one-hot works if each value has enough data.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-85971ed", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Which algorithms are sensitive to feature scaling and which aren't?", "a": "<p>Sensitive: kNN, K-means, SVM, PCA, LDA, regularised linear/logistic regression, anything trained by gradient descent (neural nets). Insensitive: decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting, Naive Bayes (per-feature distributions).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-4a0a955", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain precision and recall with an example.", "a": "<p>Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of the transactions flagged as fraud, the fraction actually fraudulent. Recall = TP/(TP+FN): of all fraudulent transactions, the fraction we flagged. Raising the threshold increases precision and lowers recall; the right balance depends on the costs of false alarms vs misses.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-05740f3", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Why is accuracy a bad metric for imbalanced data?", "a": "<p>With 1% positives, predicting “negative” for everyone gives 99% accuracy while catching nothing. Accuracy is dominated by the majority class; use precision/recall, F1, PR-AUC, balanced accuracy or MCC, or a cost-based metric.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-de67908", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What does ROC-AUC mean intuitively?", "a": "<p>The probability that the model scores a randomly chosen positive higher than a randomly chosen negative. It summarises ranking quality across all thresholds; 0.5 is random, 1.0 perfect. It is insensitive to class balance and to monotonic transformations of scores.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-d43cd38", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you prefer PR-AUC over ROC-AUC?", "a": "<p>When positives are rare and you care about performance on them (fraud, disease, defects). ROC-AUC uses FPR, whose denominator (all negatives) is huge, so many false positives barely move it; PR-AUC uses precision, which directly reflects false alarms relative to true detections.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-60f35bb", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the F1 score and when is it not appropriate?", "a": "<p>The harmonic mean of precision and recall, high only when both are high. Not appropriate when costs of FP and FN differ a lot (use Fβ or a cost function), when true negatives matter, or when you need threshold-free comparison (use AUCs).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-134042e", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Macro vs micro vs weighted averaging?", "a": "<p>Macro: average per-class metrics equally — highlights minority-class performance. Weighted: average weighted by class support. Micro: aggregate TP/FP/FN across classes — dominated by large classes; equals accuracy for single-label multiclass.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-3b9df73", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is model calibration and how do you check it?", "a": "<p>A model is calibrated if predicted probabilities match observed frequencies (of cases predicted 0.8, ~80% are positive). Check with reliability diagrams, Expected Calibration Error, Brier score. Fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression on held-out data. SVMs, Naive Bayes, boosted trees and class-weighted models often need it.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-ac6f32d", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "RMSE vs MAE — when to use which?", "a": "<p>RMSE squares errors, so it penalises large errors more and corresponds to predicting the mean; use it when big misses are especially costly. MAE treats all errors linearly, is robust to outliers and corresponds to the median. Report both: a big RMSE/MAE gap signals a few large errors.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-7550dd5", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Type I vs Type II error?", "a": "<p>Type I = false positive (rejecting a true null; flagging a legit transaction). Type II = false negative (failing to reject a false null; missing fraud). In hypothesis testing α controls Type I; power = 1 − β where β is the Type II rate.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-05daa5f", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "How do you choose a classification threshold?", "a": "<p>Not 0.5 by default. Options: maximise F1/Fβ on validation; minimise expected cost using a cost matrix (threshold = C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN) for calibrated probabilities); meet a constraint (precision ≥ 90%, or top-k capacity); Youden's J on ROC. Choose on validation data and confirm on test.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-6a5a421", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is log-loss and why might it be preferred to accuracy?", "a": "<p>The negative mean log-likelihood of true labels under predicted probabilities. It evaluates probability quality, heavily penalises confident wrong predictions, is a proper scoring rule (optimised by true probabilities), and is differentiable — accuracy ignores confidence entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-7b54aa3", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Your model has 0.95 ROC-AUC but the business says it's useless. Why might that be?", "a": "<p>Extreme imbalance (precision at usable thresholds is tiny), evaluation on the wrong population/time period (leakage or drift), metric misaligned with the decision (they need top-k precision or calibrated risk), poor performance on the segment that matters, or predictions that aren't actionable/come too late.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-c1e19b8", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is NDCG?", "a": "<p>Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain: sums graded relevance of ranked items discounted by log₂(position + 1), divided by the ideal DCG so it lies in [0, 1]. It rewards putting highly relevant items at the top; used for search and recommendation ranking evaluation (NDCG@k).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-c5f63ee", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is MCC and why is it useful?", "a": "<p>Matthews Correlation Coefficient = (TP·TN − FP·FN)/√((TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)), ranging −1 to 1. It uses all four confusion-matrix cells and is high only if the classifier does well on both classes — a reliable single metric under imbalance.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-b2e8f55", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is cross-validation and why use it?", "a": "<p>A resampling method that splits data into K folds, trains on K−1 and validates on the remaining one, rotating so every fold is validated once. It gives a lower-variance, more data-efficient estimate of generalisation than a single split, plus a spread (std) of performance, and is used for model selection and tuning.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-b4802b1", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What CV would you use for time-series data, and why?", "a": "<p>Time-ordered splits — expanding or sliding window (TimeSeriesSplit / rolling-origin backtests), optionally with a gap to prevent leakage via lag features and a horizon matching production. Random K-Fold would train on the future and validate on the past.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-4c42b95", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When should you use GroupKFold?", "a": "<p>When multiple rows belong to the same entity (patient, user, device, store) and the model will be applied to new entities. Otherwise rows from the same entity appear in both train and validation, and the model gets credit for memorising entity-specific patterns.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-4a23df8", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is nested cross-validation?", "a": "<p>Two loops: inner CV selects hyperparameters on each outer training fold; the outer CV evaluates the tuned model on held-out outer folds. It gives an unbiased estimate of the performance of the whole model-building procedure, avoiding the optimism of reporting the best inner CV score.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-09fdaf1", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Grid search vs random search?", "a": "<p>Grid search evaluates every combination of predefined values — exhaustive but exponential in the number of hyperparameters and wasteful when only a few matter. Random search samples configurations from distributions; for the same budget it explores more distinct values of each important hyperparameter and usually finds equal or better results.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-5453161", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How does Bayesian optimisation work for hyperparameter tuning?", "a": "<p>It builds a probabilistic surrogate model of the objective (Gaussian process or TPE) from past trials and uses an acquisition function (expected improvement, UCB) to pick the next configuration balancing exploration and exploitation. It needs fewer evaluations than random search for expensive models; tools: Optuna, Hyperopt, scikit-optimize.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-933e7a0", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why is stratified K-Fold important for classification?", "a": "<p>It keeps class proportions consistent across folds, reducing variance of the estimate and preventing folds with very few (or zero) minority examples, which would make metrics like recall or AUC unstable or undefined.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-f0ab170", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the bias–variance trade-off in choosing K for K-Fold?", "a": "<p>Small K trains on less data, so estimates are pessimistically biased; large K (LOOCV) trains on nearly all data (low bias) but the training sets overlap heavily, so fold scores are highly correlated and the estimate can have high variance, plus more compute. K = 5–10 balances these.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-2b6deeb", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you avoid data leakage during cross-validation?", "a": "<p>Fit every data-dependent step (imputation, scaling, encoding, feature selection, resampling, target encoding) inside the CV loop via pipelines; respect group and time structure in splits; deduplicate before splitting; never look at test data while developing.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-ddd88ff", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is successive halving / Hyperband?", "a": "<p>Multi-fidelity tuning: evaluate many configurations with a small budget (few samples, epochs or trees), keep the top 1/η, multiply the budget by η, and repeat. Hyperband runs several such brackets with different trade-offs between number of configs and starting budget. sklearn: HalvingGridSearchCV / HalvingRandomSearchCV.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-db8d15a", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between a validation curve and a learning curve?", "a": "<p>Validation curve: training and validation scores vs a hyperparameter value — find the complexity sweet spot. Learning curve: scores vs training-set size — diagnose whether more data (high variance) or more capacity (high bias) will help.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-fedf550", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "After cross-validation, which model do you deploy?", "a": "<p>Usually refit the chosen pipeline with the selected hyperparameters on all available training data (more data → better model); the CV score estimates its performance. Alternatives: an ensemble of the K fold models (common in competitions), or for time series, the model trained on the most recent window.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-d9c7a47", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Your model's performance is poor. How do you improve it?", "a": "<p>First validate the evaluation (right metric, production-like split, no leakage, baseline comparison). Then diagnose: high bias (train error high) → richer features/model, less regularisation; high variance (train ≪ validation error) → more data, regularisation, simpler model, bagging, early stopping. Do error analysis by slice and inspect worst cases; fix data/label issues; engineer domain features; tune hyperparameters; try ensembles; tune the threshold for the business metric. Validate gains online.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-5466a7b", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is stacking and how do you avoid leakage in it?", "a": "<p>Stacking trains a meta-model on the predictions of several base models. To avoid leakage the meta-model must be trained on out-of-fold predictions (each base model predicts on folds it wasn't trained on); at inference, base models trained on all data feed the meta-model. sklearn's StackingClassifier does this via its cv argument.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-ae110fb", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Bagging vs boosting vs stacking?", "a": "<p>Bagging: parallel training of the same model on bootstrap samples, averaged — reduces variance. Boosting: sequential models each fitting the ensemble's errors — reduces bias. Stacking: heterogeneous models combined by a learned meta-model — exploits complementary strengths.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-e9856dc", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What are SHAP values?", "a": "<p>Feature attributions based on Shapley values from cooperative game theory: each feature's average marginal contribution to the prediction over all feature orderings. They're additive (sum to prediction minus baseline), consistent, give local and (aggregated) global explanations, and are computed exactly and fast for trees with TreeSHAP.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-7f17567", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Permutation importance vs impurity importance vs SHAP?", "a": "<p>Impurity (MDI): fast, training-data based, biased toward high-cardinality features. Permutation: model-agnostic, measured on validation data as the score drop after shuffling a feature; can be misleading with correlated features. SHAP: per-prediction additive attributions with solid theory; global importance = mean |SHAP|; more expensive for non-tree models.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-1415a11", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is a partial dependence plot and its limitation?", "a": "<p>It shows the average model prediction as one (or two) features vary, marginalising over the others. Limitations: assumes features are independent (creates unrealistic combinations when correlated — ALE plots fix this) and averaging hides heterogeneous effects (ICE curves reveal them).</p>"}, {"id": "performance-56581bd", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you know if more data will help?", "a": "<p>Plot a learning curve: if the validation score is still rising and the train–validation gap is large at full size, more data should help (variance-limited). If both curves have plateaued close together, more data won't help — you need better features or a more expressive model.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-22fbeb1", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is error analysis and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>Systematically examining where the model fails: slicing metrics by segments, inspecting misclassified examples, categorising error types and estimating how much each category costs. It focuses effort on the fixes with the highest payoff (e.g., 40% of errors are from one mislabeled source) instead of blind tuning.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-19a2de8", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "LIME vs SHAP?", "a": "<p>LIME fits a local interpretable surrogate (sparse linear model) on perturbed samples around a prediction — model-agnostic but unstable and dependent on kernel width/sampling. SHAP has game-theoretic guarantees (local accuracy, consistency) and consistent global aggregation; KernelSHAP is like LIME with a specific weighting that recovers Shapley values.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-7467ea8", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How would you explain a complex model to a non-technical stakeholder?", "a": "<p>Lead with what it predicts and how well vs the current process (in business terms). Show the top drivers globally (SHAP summary in plain words), a couple of individual examples (“this customer is high risk mainly because of 3 missed payments and high utilisation”), the limitations/when not to trust it, and how it'll be monitored.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-eeb2efd", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "Model accuracy improved offline by 2% but the A/B test shows no lift. Why?", "a": "<p>Offline metric not aligned with the business metric; leakage or optimistic offline evaluation; train/serve skew; distribution shift between offline data and live traffic; feedback loops; the improvement is in segments that don't affect the outcome; latency increase hurting UX; underpowered experiment or novelty effects.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-9e1f269", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you NOT use an ensemble?", "a": "<p>Strict latency/memory budgets, need for simple explanations (regulated credit decisions), marginal gains not worth maintenance and debugging complexity, or when a single well-tuned model is already near the noise ceiling.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-e870e88", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain a p-value to a non-technical person.", "a": "<p>“If the new design truly made no difference, how surprising would results like ours be?” A p-value of 0.01 means results this extreme would happen only 1% of the time by random chance alone — so we doubt the “no difference” assumption. It doesn't tell us how big or important the effect is.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f38ec0e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is the Central Limit Theorem and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>The distribution of the sample mean of i.i.d. variables with finite variance approaches a normal distribution as n grows, with standard error σ/√n, regardless of the original distribution. It justifies normal-based CIs and z/t-tests on averages of non-normal metrics in A/B tests.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-dcf88ce", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Type I vs Type II error — which is worse?", "a": "<p>Type I: false positive (ship a change that doesn't work), controlled by α. Type II: false negative (miss a real improvement), rate β; power = 1 − β. Which is worse depends on costs: in drug approval Type I is worse; in early-stage product exploration a Type II error (killing a good idea) may cost more.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-49ac996", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you determine the sample size for an A/B test?", "a": "<p>Specify baseline metric value and variance, the minimum detectable effect worth acting on, α (0.05) and power (0.8); use the power formula (n ≈ 16σ²/δ² per group for α=0.05, power 0.8) or a calculator. Convert to duration with traffic, rounding up to full weeks.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f2857e2", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is statistical power and how can you increase it?", "a": "<p>The probability of detecting a true effect of a given size. Increase it with more samples, larger effects (bolder changes), lower variance (CUPED/covariate adjustment, better metrics, stratification, trimming outliers), higher α, or one-sided tests when justified.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-356b3ab", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why is peeking at A/B test results a problem?", "a": "<p>Each look is another chance to cross the significance threshold by luck, so stopping as soon as p &lt; 0.05 inflates the false-positive rate far above 5%. Pre-commit to a sample size, or use sequential methods (group sequential designs with alpha spending, always-valid p-values).</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-5661eed", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle multiple testing?", "a": "<p>Pre-specify one primary metric; correct for many comparisons with Bonferroni (α/m, conservative, controls family-wise error) or Benjamini–Hochberg (controls false discovery rate, more power). Treat segment findings as hypotheses for follow-up tests.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-dc459bc", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is sample ratio mismatch?", "a": "<p>When the observed split of users between variants differs significantly from the designed split (e.g. 50.8/49.2 with millions of users, χ² p &lt; 0.001). It signals bugs in randomisation, assignment, redirects or logging, and invalidates the results until fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-eb85d00", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What would you do if your A/B test is significant for one metric but a guardrail metric got worse?", "a": "<p>Quantify both effects with CIs and translate to business value; check whether the guardrail drop is real (SRM, novelty, segments); discuss the trade-off with stakeholders; consider iterating on the design to keep the gain without the harm; don't ship blindly on the primary metric.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-27575e3", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is Simpson's paradox?", "a": "<p>A trend in aggregated data reverses within every subgroup because of a confounding variable that changes the mix (e.g. treatment looks worse overall but better within each severity level because sicker patients got it more often). Look at stratified results and think causally.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-2754ef0", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "t-test vs z-test vs chi-square test?", "a": "<p>z-test: means/proportions with known variance or large samples. t-test: means with estimated variance (small/moderate n); Welch's version for unequal variances. χ² test: categorical counts — goodness of fit or independence (e.g. conversion vs variant), equivalent to the two-proportion z-test for 2×2 tables.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-2b8e72c", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is CUPED?", "a": "<p>Controlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data: adjust the metric with a pre-period covariate, Y' = Y − θ(X − mean X), θ = Cov(X, Y)/Var(X). It keeps the estimate unbiased (randomisation makes X balanced) while reducing variance by a factor (1 − ρ²), shortening experiments.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f74f78e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is a confidence interval? Interpret a 95% CI of [1.2%, 3.5%] lift.", "a": "<p>The procedure produces intervals that contain the true lift 95% of the time across repeated experiments. Here: the data are consistent with a true lift between 1.2% and 3.5%; since 0 is excluded the effect is significant at α=0.05, and even the low end may or may not clear the practical-significance bar.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-41d48fc", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "A test with 99% sensitivity and 99% specificity for a disease with 0.1% prevalence — P(disease | positive)?", "a": "<p>Per 100,000 people: 100 sick → 99 true positives; 99,900 healthy → 999 false positives. P = 99 / (99 + 999) ≈ 9%. The base rate dominates — the base-rate fallacy.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-a40962e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "When can't you run an A/B test, and what then?", "a": "<p>Ethical/legal constraints, network effects you can't isolate, one-off launches, too little traffic, or long-term outcomes. Use quasi-experiments: difference-in-differences, synthetic control, regression discontinuity, instrumental variables, propensity score matching, interrupted time series — with careful assumption checks.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-5a4ed26", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What are the assumptions of linear regression?", "a": "<p>Linearity in parameters; independence of errors (no autocorrelation); homoscedasticity (constant error variance); normality of errors (for inference); no perfect multicollinearity; exogeneity (errors uncorrelated with features / no omitted confounders). Mention which ones affect <i>predictions</i> (linearity, exogeneity for causal reading) vs <i>inference</i> (independence, homoscedasticity, normality).</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-a35a49d", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is homoscedasticity and how do you detect and fix heteroscedasticity?", "a": "<p>Homoscedasticity = residual variance is the same at every level of the predictors. Heteroscedasticity shows up as a funnel in residuals-vs-fitted. Detect with that plot, the scale-location plot, or Breusch–Pagan / White tests. Consequences: coefficients remain unbiased, but OLS is no longer efficient and standard errors, t-stats, p-values and CIs are wrong. Fixes: transform y (log, Box-Cox, sqrt), Weighted Least Squares with weights ∝ 1/variance, or heteroscedasticity-consistent (HC3 / White) standard errors.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-f1b69ed", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is multicollinearity? How do you detect and handle it?", "a": "<p>High correlation among predictors. It inflates the variance of coefficient estimates (unstable, sign flips, large SEs, insignificant individually but jointly significant) but does <b>not</b> hurt predictive accuracy much. Detect: correlation matrix, VIF (&gt;5–10), condition number of X. Handle: drop one of the correlated features, combine them (ratio, average, PCA), collect more data, or use Ridge which stabilises coefficients.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-13f9050", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is VIF and how is it computed?", "a": "<p>Variance Inflation Factor for feature j: regress \\(x_j\\) on all other features, take that \\(R_j^2\\), then \\(\\text{VIF}_j = 1/(1-R_j^2)\\). It is the factor by which the variance of \\(\\hat\\beta_j\\) is inflated relative to uncorrelated features. VIF = 1 none, 5 → \\(R^2_j\\)=0.8, 10 → 0.9.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-2350764", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Derive the OLS closed-form solution.", "a": "<p>Minimise \\(\\lVert y-X\\beta\\rVert^2 = y^Ty - 2\\beta^TX^Ty + \\beta^TX^TX\\beta\\). Gradient: \\(-2X^Ty + 2X^TX\\beta = 0\\) ⇒ \\(X^TX\\beta=X^Ty\\) ⇒ \\(\\hat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty\\), valid when \\(X^TX\\) is invertible (full column rank). The Hessian \\(2X^TX\\) is positive semi-definite, so it is a global minimum.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1e65f4a", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you use gradient descent instead of the normal equation?", "a": "<p>When p is large (inverting a p×p matrix is O(p³)), when n is huge or data doesn't fit in memory (mini-batch/SGD streams), for online learning, or when adding non-closed-form penalties (Lasso uses coordinate descent). Normal equation: no learning rate, no scaling needed, exact — fine for small/medium p.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-f256fd6", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we minimise squared error rather than absolute error?", "a": "<p>Squared error is differentiable everywhere and convex with a closed-form solution; it equals the MLE under Gaussian noise; it estimates the conditional mean; and it penalises large errors more. Absolute error (MAE/LAD) estimates the conditional median, is robust to outliers, corresponds to Laplace noise, but has no closed form (solved via linear programming) and a non-smooth gradient at 0.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-3e067e6", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Difference between R² and adjusted R²?", "a": "<p>R² = 1 − RSS/TSS, the share of variance explained; it never decreases when features are added (on training data). Adjusted R² = 1 − (1 − R²)(n − 1)/(n − p − 1) penalises the number of predictors, increasing only if a new feature improves the fit more than expected by chance. Use adjusted R² (or better, CV error / AIC / BIC) to compare models with different numbers of features.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-4caba6b", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Can R² be negative?", "a": "<p>On training data with an intercept, no (OLS is at least as good as the mean). On test data, or without an intercept, yes: it means the model predicts worse than a constant equal to the mean of y.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-aa5a7ec", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you interpret coefficients when y is log-transformed?", "a": "<p>log(y) = β₀ + β₁x: a one-unit increase in x multiplies y by \\(e^{\\beta_1}\\), ≈ a 100·β₁ % change for small β₁. log(y) ~ log(x): β₁ is an elasticity (1 % change in x → β₁ % change in y). y ~ log(x): a 1 % increase in x changes y by β₁/100 units.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1f8faea", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What happens if you duplicate a feature (or duplicate all rows)?", "a": "<p>Duplicate feature: perfect collinearity, \\(X^TX\\) singular; OLS has infinitely many solutions (the pseudo-inverse / sklearn splits the weight). Ridge splits it evenly. Duplicating all rows: coefficients unchanged, but standard errors shrink by √2 — falsely inflated confidence, a classic data-leakage-in-inference trap.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-06deb78", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are outliers, leverage points and influential points?", "a": "<p>Outlier: unusual y given x (large residual). High leverage: unusual x (far from the feature mean; diagonal of the hat matrix \\(H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\\)). Influential: removing it materially changes the fit — usually high leverage <i>and</i> large residual; measured by Cook's distance. OLS is sensitive because errors are squared.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-7219bbe", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What does the p-value of a coefficient mean?", "a": "<p>The probability of observing a t-statistic at least as extreme as ours if the true coefficient were 0 (and assumptions hold). Small p → evidence that the feature has a non-zero association with y given the other features. It is not the probability that the coefficient is zero, and not a measure of effect size or causality.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-2a8ba75", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is omitted-variable bias?", "a": "<p>If a variable z affects y and is correlated with an included x, leaving z out makes x's coefficient absorb z's effect: \\(E[\\hat\\beta_x] = \\beta_x + \\beta_z\\cdot\\delta\\), where δ is the slope from regressing z on x. It violates exogeneity, so coefficients are biased — no amount of data fixes it. Example: ice-cream sales vs drowning, omitted temperature.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-ae13137", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does linear regression handle categorical variables?", "a": "<p>One-hot/dummy encode with k − 1 columns (drop a baseline) to avoid the dummy-variable trap when an intercept exists; each coefficient is the difference vs the baseline. For high cardinality consider target encoding (with CV to avoid leakage) or regularisation.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-b4b5212", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Do you need to scale features for linear regression?", "a": "<p>Not for OLS predictions — the closed-form solution is scale-equivariant (coefficients just rescale). You <b>do</b> need scaling for gradient descent (faster, stable convergence), for regularised models (penalty treats all coefficients equally), and to compare coefficient magnitudes.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-bdc2829", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What if p &gt; n?", "a": "<p>\\(X^TX\\) is rank-deficient: infinitely many zero-training-error solutions, perfect overfit. Use regularisation (Ridge always has a unique solution, Lasso selects at most n features), dimensionality reduction (PCA regression, PLS), or feature selection.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1dbca18", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Explain the bias–variance of OLS and why Ridge can beat it.", "a": "<p>OLS is unbiased (under assumptions) but may have high variance with many / correlated features. Ridge introduces a small bias by shrinking coefficients, which can reduce variance by more, lowering expected test MSE. Gauss–Markov only says OLS is best among <i>unbiased</i> linear estimators.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-929ec04", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How would you check if a linear model is appropriate for your data?", "a": "<p>Scatter / pair plots, residuals vs fitted and vs each feature (no pattern), Q-Q plot, compare CV error with a flexible model (e.g., gradient boosting): if the flexible model is much better, important non-linearity/interactions are missing.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-7ed04f7", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the hat matrix and what are its properties?", "a": "<p>\\(H = X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\\), it “puts the hat on y”: \\(\\hat y = Hy\\). It is symmetric and idempotent (a projection), with trace = p + 1 (number of parameters). Diagonal elements \\(h_{ii}\\) are leverages; average leverage is (p+1)/n and points with \\(h_{ii} &gt; 2(p+1)/n\\) are high-leverage.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-d18ce42", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Correlation vs regression coefficient?", "a": "<p>Simple regression slope = \\(r\\cdot s_y/s_x\\). Correlation is symmetric, unit-free, in [−1, 1]; the slope is directional (y on x ≠ x on y) and in units of y per unit of x. In simple regression, R² = r².</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-0155188", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval?", "a": "<p>A confidence interval covers the <i>mean</i> response \\(E[y\\mid x]\\) — uncertainty only in the estimated line. A prediction interval covers a <i>new individual</i> observation — adds the irreducible noise σ², so it is always wider and does not shrink to zero with more data.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-0d34a64", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularisation?", "a": "<p>L1 (Lasso) penalises the sum of absolute coefficients, producing sparse models (exact zeros → feature selection), is non-differentiable at 0, and picks one of a group of correlated features. L2 (Ridge) penalises the sum of squares, shrinks all coefficients smoothly without zeroing, has a closed-form solution, and handles multicollinearity by spreading weight. Bayesian: L1 ↔ Laplace prior, L2 ↔ Gaussian prior.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-948b94e", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does L1 produce sparse solutions but L2 doesn't?", "a": "<p>Geometrically the L1 constraint region is a diamond (cross-polytope) whose corners lie on the axes; the elliptical RSS contours typically first touch it at a corner where some coordinates are 0. The L2 ball is smooth, so contact is generically off-axis. Analytically, the L1 subgradient is a constant ±α that can overpower a small data gradient and hold the coefficient at 0 (soft-thresholding), whereas the L2 gradient 2αβ vanishes near 0.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-cd62474", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why must you scale features before Ridge/Lasso?", "a": "<p>The penalty is applied equally to all coefficients, but a coefficient's size depends on the feature's units. Unscaled, features with large ranges get small coefficients and are barely penalised, while small-range features are penalised heavily — the regularisation becomes arbitrary. Standardise inside a pipeline so the scaler is fit only on training folds.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-21fc862", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does Ridge help with multicollinearity?", "a": "<p>With correlated features \\(X^TX\\) has tiny eigenvalues, so \\((X^TX)^{-1}\\) blows up the variance of β̂. Ridge adds α to every eigenvalue: \\((X^TX+\\alpha I)\\) is always invertible and well-conditioned, which stabilises the coefficients (at the cost of some bias) and splits weight among correlated features.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-dfd76b4", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose Elastic Net over Lasso?", "a": "<p>When features are strongly correlated (Lasso arbitrarily keeps one and is unstable; Elastic Net's L2 part gives a grouping effect), and when p ≫ n (Lasso can select at most n features). It keeps Lasso's sparsity while gaining Ridge's stability.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-8494234", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose α?", "a": "<p>Cross-validation over a log-spaced grid (RidgeCV, LassoCV, ElasticNetCV or GridSearchCV with the scaler inside a Pipeline). Pick the α with the lowest CV error or, for a simpler model, the largest α within one standard error of the minimum. Information criteria (AIC/BIC via LassoLarsIC) are faster alternatives.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-07a2870", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What happens as α → 0 and α → ∞?", "a": "<p>α → 0: the solution approaches OLS (high variance if overfitting). α → ∞: all coefficients go to 0 and the model predicts the mean (intercept only) — maximum bias. In between lies the bias–variance sweet spot.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-4b51135", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is the intercept regularised? Why not?", "a": "<p>No. Penalising it would make predictions depend on the arbitrary origin of y (shifting y by a constant would change the fit). sklearn centres the data and fits the intercept separately.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-a1af7f9", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Explain regularisation from a Bayesian perspective.", "a": "<p>The posterior ∝ likelihood × prior. With Gaussian noise, the negative log-posterior is RSS/(2σ²) − log p(β). A Gaussian prior \\(\\beta_j\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tau^2)\\) gives the penalty \\(\\sum\\beta_j^2/(2\\tau^2)\\) → Ridge with α = σ²/τ². A Laplace prior gives an L1 penalty → Lasso. So the MAP estimate equals the regularised solution; a tighter prior (small τ) = larger α.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-5387227", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Lasso selected different features on two bootstrap samples. Why, and what would you do?", "a": "<p>Lasso is unstable under correlation: among correlated features it picks one nearly at random, and small data changes flip the choice. Remedies: Elastic Net, stability selection (fraction of bootstrap fits in which each feature is selected), group Lasso for known groups, or clustering correlated features first. And don't over-interpret Lasso's selection as causal importance.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-2d62620", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does regularisation reduce bias or variance?", "a": "<p>It increases bias and reduces variance. It helps when the variance reduction outweighs the added bias — typically with many features, small n, noisy data or collinearity.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-d09b776", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the relation between C in LogisticRegression and α?", "a": "<p>C is the inverse of regularisation strength (C ≈ 1/α, scaled by n depending on the formulation). Large C = weak regularisation; small C = strong regularisation. Same idea in SVMs.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-7eaf74f", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why can't Lasso be solved with the normal equation or plain gradient descent?", "a": "<p>|β| isn't differentiable at 0, so there's no closed form and the gradient is undefined at exactly the points we care about. We use coordinate descent with soft-thresholding (sklearn), LARS (which traces the whole path), proximal gradient (ISTA/FISTA), or subgradient methods (which don't produce exact zeros).</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-43a7121", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Name other forms of regularisation in ML.", "a": "<p>Early stopping, dropout, weight decay, data augmentation, tree constraints (max_depth, min_samples_leaf, pruning), shrinkage/learning rate and subsampling in boosting, limiting the number of features (feature selection), ensembling, label smoothing, adding noise.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-b6220d3", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is it called “regression” if it's used for classification?", "a": "<p>It regresses the log-odds of the positive class on the features (a generalised linear model with a logit link). It outputs a continuous probability; classification comes from thresholding that probability.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-d5cbbdb", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do we use the sigmoid function?", "a": "<p>It maps any real number to (0, 1), is monotonic and differentiable with a convenient derivative σ(1−σ), and is the inverse of the logit, so it follows naturally from modelling log-odds linearly (also the canonical link for the Bernoulli distribution).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-6558444", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why not use MSE as the loss for logistic regression?", "a": "<p>MSE through a sigmoid is non-convex (local minima) and its gradient is multiplied by σ'(z), which is tiny when the prediction is confidently wrong, so learning stalls. Log-loss is the negative Bernoulli log-likelihood, convex in w, and its gradient (p̂ − y)x is large exactly when the model is wrong.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-c16b38c", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How do you interpret a logistic regression coefficient?", "a": "<p>A one-unit increase in x_j changes the log-odds by w_j, i.e., multiplies the odds by e^{w_j} (odds ratio), holding other features constant. E.g., w = 0.4 → odds × 1.49 (+49%). The change in probability depends on the baseline probability.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-901404f", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is the decision boundary of logistic regression linear?", "a": "<p>Yes: p̂ = 0.5 ⇔ wᵀx + b = 0, a hyperplane in feature space (any threshold t gives wᵀx + b = logit(t), still a hyperplane). It becomes non-linear in the original space only if you add non-linear features (polynomials, splines, interactions).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-f8a706d", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are the assumptions of logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Binary/categorical outcome; independent observations; linearity between continuous features and the log-odds; little multicollinearity; no extreme influential outliers; sufficiently large sample (≈10+ events per predictor). It does not assume normality, homoscedasticity or linear x–y relationships.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-7f77828", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does logistic regression have a closed-form solution?", "a": "<p>No — setting the gradient Xᵀ(σ(Xw) − y) = 0 gives non-linear equations. It is solved iteratively: Newton–Raphson / IRLS, L-BFGS, coordinate descent (liblinear), or SGD/SAGA. Convexity guarantees a global optimum (if it exists).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-db9f5cf", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What happens with perfectly separable data?", "a": "<p>The likelihood keeps increasing as ‖w‖ → ∞ (probabilities pushed to exactly 0/1), so the MLE doesn't exist; coefficients and SEs blow up and solvers fail to converge. Fix: regularisation (default L2 in sklearn), Firth's penalised likelihood, or removing the perfectly predictive (often leaking!) feature.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-c489fda", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance with logistic regression?", "a": "<p>class_weight=\"balanced\" (or custom weights), resampling (undersample majority, SMOTE oversampling inside CV), and — most importantly — moving the decision threshold based on business costs or F-score on a validation set. Evaluate with PR-AUC, recall/precision, not accuracy. Note weighting/resampling biases predicted probabilities; recalibrate if you need true probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-4ea0f0c", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "One-vs-rest vs multinomial (softmax) logistic regression?", "a": "<p>OvR trains K independent binary models; simple and parallel, but probabilities aren't jointly modelled. Multinomial fits one model with a softmax over K linear scores and cross-entropy loss — coherent probabilities summing to 1, usually better calibrated; it's sklearn's default with lbfgs.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-2943110", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Logistic regression vs linear SVM?", "a": "<p>Both are linear classifiers with L2 regularisation. LR minimises log-loss (every point contributes, gives probabilities); SVM minimises hinge loss (only margin violators / support vectors matter, no probabilities without Platt scaling, maximises the margin). SVM can be more robust to points far from the boundary; LR is better when you need calibrated probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-b38299f", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Logistic regression vs Naive Bayes?", "a": "<p>LR is discriminative (models P(y|x) directly); NB is generative (models P(x|y)P(y) with a feature-independence assumption). With little data NB converges faster and can win; with more data LR typically achieves lower asymptotic error (Ng & Jordan). In fact Gaussian NB with shared variances implies a linear (logistic-form) decision boundary.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-60e0981", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the log-loss of a model that always predicts 0.5? What's a good log-loss?", "a": "<p>ln 2 ≈ 0.693 for any labels. A useful baseline is predicting the base rate π: loss = −[π ln π + (1−π) ln(1−π)] (e.g., 0.199 for π = 5%). A model is only useful if it beats that baseline.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-d1d354a", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the gradient of the log-loss and how does it compare with linear regression?", "a": "<p>∇L = (1/n) Xᵀ(σ(Xw) − y). It has the same structure as linear regression's Xᵀ(Xw − y) — residual times input — because both are GLMs using their canonical link. The Hessian is XᵀSX with S = diag(p(1−p)), used by Newton/IRLS.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-8dcdc37", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Are logistic regression probabilities calibrated?", "a": "<p>Generally yes when the model is well specified and unweighted, because maximising log-likelihood is a proper scoring rule. Strong regularisation, class weights, resampling or misspecification can miscalibrate it; check with a reliability diagram / Brier score and fix with Platt or isotonic calibration.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-71e5261", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How would you capture non-linear effects in logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Add polynomial or spline basis functions (SplineTransformer), bin continuous variables (WoE binning in credit scoring), log/ratio transforms, interaction terms, or kernel approximations (Nystroem + LR). If many interactions matter, move to tree ensembles.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-cab95ed", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which metric would you use to evaluate a logistic regression model?", "a": "<p>Depends on the goal: ROC-AUC for ranking quality, PR-AUC/F1/recall@precision for rare positives, log-loss/Brier for probability quality, calibration plots when probabilities drive decisions, and a business cost metric at the chosen threshold. Accuracy only with balanced classes and symmetric costs.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-ec1522b", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is kNN called a lazy learner?", "a": "<p>It does no generalisation during training — it just stores the data. All computation (distance calculations, voting) happens at prediction time. Contrast with eager learners (e.g., logistic regression) that build a model up front and discard the training data.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-a284d54", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does the choice of k affect the model?", "a": "<p>Small k → flexible, low bias, high variance (overfits noise; k = 1 has zero training error). Large k → smoother, higher bias, lower variance; k = n predicts the majority class. Choose by cross-validation; prefer odd k for binary problems.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-413d9e7", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do you need feature scaling for kNN?", "a": "<p>Distances are dominated by features with the largest ranges, so unscaled kNN effectively ignores small-range features. Standardisation or min–max scaling gives each feature comparable influence. Fit the scaler on training data only.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-e1fa505", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the curse of dimensionality and how does it affect kNN?", "a": "<p>In high dimensions data become sparse and pairwise distances concentrate (nearest ≈ farthest), so neighbourhoods are no longer local and the vote is close to random; you need exponentially more data to keep the same density. Mitigate with feature selection, dimensionality reduction (PCA, embeddings) or metric learning.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-e111371", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the time and space complexity of kNN?", "a": "<p>Training O(1) time (O(n·d) memory to store data). Brute-force prediction O(n·d) per query plus O(n log k) to select neighbours. KD-trees/ball trees cut queries to ~O(log n) in low dimensions but degrade toward brute force as d grows; approximate methods (HNSW, FAISS) trade exactness for speed.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-8a557cc", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does kNN handle categorical features and missing values?", "a": "<p>Categoricals need encoding (one-hot) or a suitable metric (Hamming, Gower for mixed types). Missing values must be imputed first (or use a distance that ignores missing coordinates, like sklearn's nan_euclidean used by KNNImputer).</p>"}, {"id": "knn-d73349d", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "kNN for regression — how?", "a": "<p>Predict the (optionally distance-weighted) mean of the k neighbours' targets. Predictions are piecewise constant and can't extrapolate beyond the range of training targets.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-d781a77", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the distance metric?", "a": "<p>By data type and CV: Euclidean for scaled continuous data, Manhattan for high-dimensional or outlier-prone data, cosine for text/embeddings, Hamming/Jaccard for binary/sets, Mahalanobis for correlated features, Gower for mixed data.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-645ec3a", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the asymptotic error of 1-NN?", "a": "<p>Cover & Hart (1967): as n → ∞, the 1-NN error rate is at most twice the Bayes (optimal) error. With k → ∞ and k/n → 0, kNN is consistent (approaches Bayes error).</p>"}, {"id": "knn-1426756", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "kNN vs K-Means?", "a": "<p>Different problems: kNN is supervised (classification/regression) using k labelled neighbours; K-Means is unsupervised clustering into K groups by iteratively updating centroids. Both rely on distance and need scaling.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-c292aef", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Where is kNN used in practice today?", "a": "<p>Similarity search and recommendations (“customers like you”), vector databases / RAG retrieval with approximate NN, imputation (KNNImputer), anomaly detection (distance to k-th neighbour, LOF), and as a quick baseline on small datasets.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-b888656", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does kNN deal with imbalanced classes?", "a": "<p>Poorly by default — the majority class tends to win the vote in overlap regions. Use distance weighting, resampling, class-weighted votes, or tune a threshold on the vote fraction (predict_proba).</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-f2e9817", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is Naive Bayes called “naive”?", "a": "<p>Because it assumes all features are conditionally independent given the class, so P(x₁,…,x_d | y) = ∏ P(x_j | y). This is rarely true (words like “New” and “York” co-occur), but it makes the model tractable with little data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-366b865", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does Naive Bayes work well even though the independence assumption is violated?", "a": "<p>Classification only needs the correct class to have the highest score, not accurate probabilities. Dependencies often affect all classes similarly or cancel out, so the argmax is still right even if the probabilities are badly miscalibrated (Domingos & Pazzani). Its low variance also helps on small/high-dimensional data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-05632c4", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is Laplace smoothing and why is it needed?", "a": "<p>Adding a pseudo-count α (1 for Laplace) to each feature-class count so no conditional probability is zero. Without it, a single unseen word in a test document makes the whole class likelihood zero regardless of all other evidence.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-9c0d22d", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between Multinomial, Bernoulli and Gaussian NB?", "a": "<p>Multinomial models counts/frequencies (how many times each word appears). Bernoulli models binary presence/absence and explicitly penalises absent features — good for short texts. Gaussian models continuous features with a per-class normal distribution.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-df1793e", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is Naive Bayes generative or discriminative? What does that mean?", "a": "<p>Generative: it models the joint distribution via P(x|y)P(y) and could in principle generate data. Discriminative models (logistic regression, SVM) model P(y|x) or the boundary directly. Generative models need less data and handle missing features naturally; discriminative ones usually win with lots of data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-bfa5a15", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we use log probabilities?", "a": "<p>Multiplying hundreds of probabilities &lt; 1 underflows to 0 in floating point. Log turns products into sums, is monotonic (argmax unchanged), and is numerically stable; normalise with log-sum-exp when you need actual probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-6ef5cae", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Are Naive Bayes probabilities reliable?", "a": "<p>Not usually — correlated features are counted as independent evidence, pushing probabilities toward 0 or 1. Use NB for ranking/classification; if you need probabilities, calibrate (CalibratedClassifierCV with isotonic or sigmoid).</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-db41a7e", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does Naive Bayes handle continuous and missing features?", "a": "<p>Continuous: GaussianNB, or discretise into bins and use categorical/multinomial NB, or kernel density estimates. Missing: simply drop that feature's term from the sum for that sample (a generative advantage) — though sklearn requires imputation.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-c60af7b", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Show that Multinomial NB is a linear classifier.", "a": "<p>log P(y=1|x) − log P(y=0|x) = log(π₁/π₀) + Σ_j x_j·log(θ_{j1}/θ_{j0}). This is b + wᵀx with w_j = log(θ_{j1}/θ_{j0}), linear in the counts x — the same functional form as logistic regression but with weights estimated generatively rather than by maximising conditional likelihood.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-b4d3637", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you use Naive Bayes over logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Very small training sets, extremely high-dimensional sparse text, need for millisecond training/online updates, or as a quick baseline. With ample data and correlated features, logistic regression or boosting typically perform better.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-a4160a2", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Compute P(spam | “free”) given P(spam)=0.2, P(free|spam)=0.5, P(free|ham)=0.05.", "a": "<p>Numerator 0.2 × 0.5 = 0.10; evidence = 0.10 + 0.8 × 0.05 = 0.14; posterior = 0.10 / 0.14 ≈ 0.714.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-74ec7ed", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What are support vectors?", "a": "<p>Training points that lie on the margin boundary or violate it (inside the margin or misclassified) — those with non-zero dual coefficients α. They alone define the hyperplane; removing any other point leaves the solution unchanged.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-b030163", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the role of C in SVM?", "a": "<p>C is the penalty on slack (margin violations), i.e. inverse regularisation. Large C → fewer violations, narrower margin, risk of overfitting outliers. Small C → wider margin, more violations tolerated, smoother boundary, more bias.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-618fe27", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What does gamma do in an RBF kernel?", "a": "<p>γ controls how far a single training point's influence reaches: K = exp(−γ‖x − z‖²). Large γ → narrow bumps, very wiggly boundary, overfitting; small γ → broad influence, smooth almost-linear boundary, underfitting. Tune jointly with C.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-2251502", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Explain the kernel trick.", "a": "<p>Algorithms that depend on data only through dot products (like the SVM dual) can replace xᵢᵀxⱼ with a kernel K(xᵢ, xⱼ) = φ(xᵢ)ᵀφ(xⱼ). This implicitly operates in a high (even infinite) dimensional feature space where data may be linearly separable, without ever computing φ — cost depends on n, not on the dimension of φ.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-125bd7b", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why maximise the margin?", "a": "<p>A larger margin means the classifier is robust to small perturbations and, from statistical learning theory, has lower capacity (VC-dimension bound depends on margin, not dimension), giving better generalisation guarantees. It also yields a unique solution among all separating hyperplanes.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-d0841bf", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Hinge loss vs log loss — compare SVM and logistic regression.", "a": "<p>Hinge loss is zero for points correctly classified beyond the margin, so only support vectors matter → sparse solution, no probabilities. Log loss is never zero, every point contributes, and it yields calibrated probabilities. Both are convex surrogates for 0-1 loss and use L2 regularisation; performance is often similar for linear problems.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-d8db86c", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why is feature scaling important for SVM?", "a": "<p>The margin is measured in Euclidean distance and the RBF kernel depends on ‖x − z‖, so large-scale features dominate both. Unscaled data also slows down solvers. Standardise inside a pipeline.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-2c797b6", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does SVM handle multiclass classification?", "a": "<p>Via decomposition: one-vs-one (K(K−1)/2 pairwise classifiers, majority vote — sklearn SVC) or one-vs-rest (K classifiers — LinearSVC). Crammer–Singer is a joint multiclass formulation.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-f0194b9", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why solve the dual instead of the primal?", "a": "<p>The dual depends on data only via dot products, enabling kernels; its size depends on n (not on the feature dimension); and the solution is sparse in α (support vectors). For linear SVMs with huge n and moderate d, the primal (LinearSVC, SGD) is actually faster.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-b0d293a", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you not use an SVM?", "a": "<p>Very large datasets (kernel SVM scales ~n²–n³), lots of noise/overlap (many support vectors), when calibrated probabilities or interpretability are needed, or with heterogeneous tabular data where gradient boosting typically wins.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-e5ad7c2", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What makes a function a valid kernel?", "a": "<p>Mercer's condition: K must be symmetric and positive semi-definite — every Gram matrix it produces has non-negative eigenvalues — so it corresponds to an inner product in some feature space. Sums, products and positive scalings of valid kernels are valid kernels.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-ec4d0c2", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you get probabilities from an SVM?", "a": "<p>Platt scaling: fit a logistic regression on the SVM decision values using cross-validation (SVC(probability=True) or CalibratedClassifierCV). Isotonic regression is an alternative with more data.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-77c7eaf", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does a decision tree decide where to split?", "a": "<p>It evaluates every feature and candidate threshold and chooses the split that maximises the impurity decrease (Gini/entropy for classification, variance/MSE reduction for regression), weighted by child sizes. It does this greedily at each node.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-32271a5", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Gini impurity vs entropy — which and why?", "a": "<p>Both measure node impurity and are maximal at a uniform class mix. Gini = 1 − Σp² (no logarithm, slightly faster); entropy = −Σp log p (information-theoretic, slightly more balanced splits). They produce nearly identical trees; Gini is sklearn's default.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-5b79003", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Compute the Gini of a node with 30 positives and 10 negatives.", "a": "<p>p = 0.75, 0.25 → 1 − (0.5625 + 0.0625) = 0.375. Entropy = −0.75 log₂0.75 − 0.25 log₂0.25 ≈ 0.811.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-4739605", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do decision trees overfit and how do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>They can keep splitting until every leaf is pure, fitting noise. Prevent with pre-pruning (max_depth, min_samples_leaf, min_samples_split, max_leaf_nodes, min_impurity_decrease), post-pruning (cost-complexity ccp_alpha), or by ensembling (bagging/RF, boosting).</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-1aad782", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do regression trees work?", "a": "<p>Same recursive partitioning, but splits minimise the weighted MSE (variance) of children, and leaves predict the mean of their targets (median with MAE). The result is a piecewise-constant function that can't extrapolate.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-b0c7b46", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Do decision trees need feature scaling? Why?", "a": "<p>No. A split only depends on the ordering of a feature's values, which monotonic transformations (scaling, log) preserve. The same goes for tree ensembles.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-58e2832", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What's the difference between pre-pruning and post-pruning?", "a": "<p>Pre-pruning stops growth early with constraints (cheap, but may stop before a useful deeper split — horizon effect). Post-pruning grows the full tree and then removes subtrees whose complexity isn't justified by error reduction, e.g. minimal cost-complexity pruning R(T) + α|T| with α chosen by CV.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-0cea029", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why are decision trees unstable / high variance?", "a": "<p>Greedy hierarchical splits: a small change in data can change the root split, which changes everything below. This is why averaging many trees (bagging, random forests) dramatically reduces variance.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-105275a", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why is impurity-based (MDI) feature importance biased?", "a": "<p>It's computed on training data and favours features with many possible split points (continuous or high-cardinality), which can reduce impurity by chance — even random IDs get importance. Prefer permutation importance on held-out data or SHAP values.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-63644b7", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do trees handle categorical variables and missing values?", "a": "<p>In theory a tree can split a categorical feature into subsets; sklearn needs encoding (ordinal is often fine for trees; one-hot can fragment). LightGBM/CatBoost handle categories natively. Missing values: C4.5 distributes fractionally, CART uses surrogate splits, sklearn ≥ 1.3 / XGBoost / LightGBM learn a default direction for NaNs.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-8224256", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the time complexity of training a decision tree?", "a": "<p>Roughly O(d · n log n) per level with presorting (sort each feature once, then sweep thresholds), giving O(d · n log n · depth) overall; balanced trees have depth ≈ log n. Prediction is O(depth).</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-ec869da", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why can't a tree extrapolate and why does that matter?", "a": "<p>Leaves output constants learned from training targets, so beyond the training range the prediction is flat. For trending data (time series with growth, prices rising) trees underpredict — detrend, model differences, or use linear components.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-1d563f9", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why isn't misclassification error used to grow trees?", "a": "<p>It's piecewise linear (not strictly concave) in class proportions, so many splits that improve purity produce zero change in error and the greedy search gets stuck. Gini and entropy are strictly concave, rewarding any increase in purity.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-50d1e7f", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How would you explain a decision tree to a business stakeholder?", "a": "<p>Walk a path as a rule: “If tenure &lt; 6 months AND monthly charges &gt; ₹1,500 AND no contract → 72% churn probability (based on 840 customers).” Show the top few levels, leaf sizes and probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-7821fbb", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does a Random Forest work?", "a": "<p>It trains many decision trees, each on a bootstrap sample of the rows, and at every split considers only a random subset of features. Predictions are averaged (regression) or majority-voted / probability-averaged (classification). Bootstrapping + feature subsampling decorrelate trees so averaging reduces variance.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-8d66c52", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does bagging reduce variance but not bias?", "a": "<p>The average of B identically distributed estimators has the same expectation (so bias is unchanged) but variance ρσ² + (1−ρ)σ²/B, which is smaller than σ². Hence bagging helps high-variance, low-bias learners like deep trees, and does little for stable, high-bias models like linear regression.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-fdb28c1", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does RF use a random subset of features at each split?", "a": "<p>To decorrelate the trees. Without it, strong predictors dominate the top splits of every tree, making them similar (high ρ), and averaging correlated trees removes little variance. Random subsets force diversity, lowering ρ and the ensemble variance.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-af26503", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Can a Random Forest overfit by adding more trees?", "a": "<p>No — adding trees reduces the variance term and the generalisation error converges to a limit. Individual deep trees overfit, and the forest can still overfit via tree depth/leaf size on noisy data, but n_estimators itself isn't an overfitting knob (it's a compute knob).</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-f23f283", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the out-of-bag error?", "a": "<p>Each tree leaves out ~36.8% of rows. Predicting each row with only the trees that didn't train on it and scoring those predictions gives an internal, nearly unbiased estimate of test error — like CV for free. Invalid with time ordering or grouped data.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-5e3aa9b", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are the most important RF hyperparameters?", "a": "<p>max_features (controls decorrelation vs tree strength), n_estimators (enough to stabilise), and tree size controls (min_samples_leaf, max_depth). Also max_samples, class_weight for imbalance. RF is fairly insensitive compared with boosting.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-3cc6a0a", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Random Forest vs Gradient Boosting — when would you choose each?", "a": "<p>RF: quick robust baseline, noisy labels, little tuning time, parallel training, lower risk of overfitting. Boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM/CatBoost): usually best accuracy on tabular data when tuned with early stopping, handles bias better, but sequential and more sensitive to hyperparameters and noise.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-2d95780", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why is impurity-based feature importance unreliable?", "a": "<p>It's computed on training data and rewards features offering many split points — continuous or high-cardinality columns (even random IDs) — and it splits credit arbitrarily among correlated features. Use permutation importance on validation data, drop-column importance, or SHAP.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-874c4fe", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does Random Forest need feature scaling or handle missing values?", "a": "<p>No scaling needed (tree splits are order-based). Missing values: sklearn's RF supports NaN since v1.4; otherwise impute (median + missing-indicator). Categoricals need encoding in sklearn.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-0cdd8c6", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance in Random Forest?", "a": "<p>class_weight=\"balanced\" or \"balanced_subsample\" (weights recomputed per bootstrap), balanced random forest (undersample majority in each bootstrap — imblearn), threshold tuning on predict_proba, and evaluation with PR-AUC.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-8fb60e7", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does a RF compute predicted probabilities?", "a": "<p>sklearn averages each tree's leaf class proportions (soft voting). These tend to be under-confident near 0/1 (averaging pulls toward the middle); calibrate with isotonic/sigmoid if probabilities matter.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-2513212", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why are fully grown trees used in RF?", "a": "<p>Averaging fixes variance, not bias, so each base learner should have low bias. Deep trees are low-bias/high-variance — the ideal ingredient. Limiting depth would add bias that averaging can't remove (though min_samples_leaf can help on noisy targets).</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-5ad8d8e", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is ExtraTrees and how is it different?", "a": "<p>Extremely Randomised Trees pick split thresholds at random (choosing the best among random candidates) and by default train on the full dataset (no bootstrap). More randomness → lower variance, faster, slightly more bias.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-8466e49", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is boosting and how does it differ from bagging?", "a": "<p>Boosting trains weak learners sequentially, each focusing on the errors of the current ensemble, and combines them with weights — mainly reducing bias. Bagging trains strong learners independently on bootstrap samples and averages them — mainly reducing variance. Boosting is sequential and can overfit with too many rounds; bagging is parallel and doesn't.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-cc0d226", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Explain how gradient boosting works.", "a": "<p>Initialise with a constant minimising the loss. Repeatedly compute pseudo-residuals = negative gradient of the loss w.r.t. current predictions, fit a small regression tree to them, set optimal leaf values, and add the tree scaled by the learning rate. It's gradient descent in function space; for squared error the pseudo-residuals are the ordinary residuals.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-a90dfca", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why “gradient” boosting? What is the gradient taken with respect to?", "a": "<p>With respect to the model's predictions F(xᵢ) for each training point, not the tree parameters. The vector of negative gradients is the steepest-descent direction in prediction space; each tree approximates that direction as a function of x so it generalises to new points.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-4f5e149", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does AdaBoost work?", "a": "<p>Start with uniform sample weights; fit a weak learner; compute its weighted error ε and weight α = ½ ln((1−ε)/ε); increase weights of misclassified samples by e^{α} and decrease the others, normalise; repeat. The final classifier is the sign of the α-weighted vote. It minimises exponential loss stage-wise.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-4c14803", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why is AdaBoost sensitive to outliers?", "a": "<p>Exponential loss grows exponentially with the negative margin, so persistently misclassified points (mislabelled or outliers) get ever-larger weights and the ensemble contorts itself to fit them. Gradient boosting with log-loss or Huber loss is more robust.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-9f0c7b4", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the role of the learning rate in boosting?", "a": "<p>It scales each tree's contribution (shrinkage). Lower values make each step smaller, requiring more trees but reducing overfitting and usually improving test performance. Tune it together with the number of trees via early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-c4f67d0", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Can gradient boosting overfit? How do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>Yes — with too many rounds, deep trees or a large learning rate it fits noise. Prevent with early stopping on a validation set, lower learning rate, shallower trees / fewer leaves, min samples per leaf, row and column subsampling, and L1/L2 penalties on leaf weights.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-1726620", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why are shallow trees used as base learners in boosting?", "a": "<p>Boosting reduces bias by adding many small corrections; each learner only needs to be slightly better than chance. Shallow trees are low variance, fast, and depth controls the maximum interaction order. Deep trees would overfit quickly and make the sequential correction noisy.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-da65d09", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the initial prediction F₀ for regression and for binary classification?", "a": "<p>The constant minimising the loss: the mean of y for squared error (median for absolute error) and the log-odds of the positive rate, log(p/(1−p)), for log-loss.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-1c330a6", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is stochastic gradient boosting?", "a": "<p>Friedman's variant: each tree is fit on a random subsample of rows (without replacement, e.g. 50–80%). It injects randomness that reduces variance and correlation between trees, speeds training, and typically improves generalisation. Column subsampling extends the idea.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-ebc5d27", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Gradient boosting vs Random Forest: which handles noisy data better and why?", "a": "<p>Random Forest — averaging independent deep trees is robust, and adding trees doesn't overfit. Boosting keeps fitting residuals, which on noisy labels are largely noise, so it needs careful regularisation and early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-b89e9e9", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the number of trees?", "a": "<p>Early stopping: monitor a validation metric each round and stop when it hasn't improved for k rounds (e.g. 50), keeping the best iteration. With CV, take the average best iteration across folds for the final refit on all data.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-23a05f1", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How can boosting produce prediction intervals?", "a": "<p>Train models with quantile loss at, say, α = 0.05 and 0.95 (GradientBoostingRegressor(loss=\"quantile\"), LightGBM objective=\"quantile\"), or use conformal prediction on top of any regressor for distribution-free coverage guarantees.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-fc58c90", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Explain XGBoost to a non-technical stakeholder.", "a": "<p>It's a team of simple yes/no rule-trees built one after another. Each new tree studies the mistakes the team is still making and corrects them a little. After hundreds of small corrections the team is very accurate, and built-in limits stop it from memorising quirks in the data.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-87f8759", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose XGBoost over LightGBM, and vice versa?", "a": "<p>XGBoost for small-to-medium data where its level-wise, balanced trees overfit less and where its ecosystem features help (monotonic constraints, mature SHAP and Spark support). LightGBM when data is large, from millions of rows up, and training speed and memory dominate, or with very sparse high-dimensional features. On most datasets accuracy is within noise once both are tuned.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-9435ceb", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why can't XGBoost extrapolate, and what do you do about it?", "a": "<p>Every prediction is a sum of leaf values learned from training rows, so outputs stay within the range seen in training. A target that keeps growing (sales trend) gets a flat forecast. Fixes: model the differenced or detrended target, add trend features and predict the residual, or combine with a linear or time-series model.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-02fac2b", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does XGBoost need feature scaling or one-hot encoding?", "a": "<p>No scaling: trees split on thresholds, so monotonic transforms don't change the result. For categoricals you can one-hot low-cardinality columns, use enable_categorical=True with pandas category dtype, or target-encode carefully. For many high-cardinality categoricals, CatBoost is usually easier.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-671b499", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What makes XGBoost different from classic gradient boosting?", "a": "<p>A regularised objective (γ per leaf, L2/L1 on leaf weights), second-order (Newton) optimisation with gradients and hessians giving closed-form leaf weights and gains, sparsity-aware handling of missing values, weighted quantile sketch / histogram split finding, column subsampling, built-in pruning via γ, and systems work: parallel, cache-aware, out-of-core and GPU training.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-3669d9c", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What do gamma, lambda and min_child_weight do in XGBoost?", "a": "<p>gamma (min_split_loss): minimum gain required to make a split — prunes weak splits. lambda (reg_lambda): L2 penalty on leaf weights, shrinking them toward 0 and appearing in the denominator H + λ. min_child_weight: minimum sum of hessians in a child; blocks tiny or ultra-confident leaves. All three make trees more conservative.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-91c73e5", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does XGBoost handle missing values?", "a": "<p>During training each split tries sending all missing values left and then right and keeps the direction with higher gain — a learned default direction per node. At inference, missing values follow that direction. No imputation needed (though missingness that's informative is captured automatically).</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-89606d8", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which hyperparameters would you tune first for XGBoost/LightGBM?", "a": "<p>Learning rate + number of trees (via early stopping), then tree complexity (max_depth / num_leaves, min_child_weight / min_child_samples), then sampling (subsample, colsample_bytree), then regularisation (lambda, alpha, gamma). Finish by lowering the learning rate and re-fitting with early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-cc4041a", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance in XGBoost?", "a": "<p>scale_pos_weight ≈ negatives/positives (or sample weights), an imbalance-aware eval metric (aucpr) for early stopping, threshold tuning on validation data, and possibly max_delta_step for extreme imbalance with log-loss. Recalibrate probabilities if you reweighted.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-e8edf4e", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why does XGBoost use the second derivative?", "a": "<p>A second-order approximation gives a Newton step: leaf values account for curvature (−G/H) rather than using a fixed line search, converging faster and more accurately, and it provides a unified closed-form gain for any twice-differentiable loss, making custom objectives easy (just supply g and h).</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-d9c2772", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which feature importance would you trust from XGBoost?", "a": "<p>Built-in types disagree: weight (# splits) favours continuous features, gain (average loss reduction) is better but still biased, cover is sample-count based. For reliable global and local explanations use SHAP (TreeSHAP), or permutation importance on held-out data.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-998842f", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What are monotonic constraints and why use them?", "a": "<p>They force the model's prediction to be non-decreasing (or non-increasing) in a chosen feature, e.g. credit risk must not decrease as utilisation rises. They encode domain knowledge, improve generalisation/robustness, and satisfy regulators. Set via monotone_constraints in XGBoost/LightGBM.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-a1f4076", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do gradient-boosted trees usually beat deep learning on tabular data?", "a": "<p>Tabular features are heterogeneous, often irregular with sharp thresholds and uninformative columns; trees handle mixed scales, missing values and categorical splits natively, are robust to irrelevant features, and need less data and tuning. Neural nets have smooth inductive biases better suited to images, text and audio.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-afb99e2", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Derive the optimal leaf weight in XGBoost.", "a": "<p>For leaf j, the second-order objective is G_j w + ½(H_j + λ) w² (+ γ). Differentiating and setting to zero: G_j + (H_j + λ) w = 0 ⇒ w* = −G_j / (H_j + λ). Plugging back gives the leaf score −½ G_j²/(H_j + λ), from which the split gain formula follows.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-fd25ac4", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is LightGBM usually faster than XGBoost?", "a": "<p>Four things: it bins features into histograms (and computes one child's histogram by subtraction), it grows trees leaf-wise so it reaches good loss with fewer splits, GOSS can skip many well-predicted rows, and EFB bundles sparse columns. Together these cut both rows and columns scanned. XGBoost's hist mode has closed much of the gap, but LightGBM is still typically faster and lighter on very large data.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-d2eb193", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you stop LightGBM overfitting on a small dataset?", "a": "<p>Lower num_leaves (e.g. 15–31) and set max_depth, raise min_child_samples, add subsample/colsample_bytree and L1/L2 regularisation, lower max_bin, use a small learning rate with early stopping, and validate with cross-validation. If it still overfits, XGBoost, CatBoost or Random Forest may simply suit the data better.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-0efc5c5", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What's the relationship between num_leaves and max_depth?", "a": "<p>A balanced tree of depth d has at most 2^d leaves. Because LightGBM grows leaf-wise, num_leaves is the real complexity control. Keep num_leaves below 2^max_depth (e.g. depth 7 → ≤ 127), otherwise trees can become very deep and specific.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-655775a", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does LightGBM handle categorical features?", "a": "<p>Mark them as pandas category (or pass categorical_feature). For each split it sorts categories by their gradient statistics and finds the best cut in that order, giving a many-vs-many partition without one-hot encoding. Regularise rare categories with cat_smooth, min_data_per_group and max_cat_threshold.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-e17f830", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose LightGBM over XGBoost or CatBoost?", "a": "<p>Large data (millions of rows or many features), frequent retraining, big hyperparameter searches, sparse high-dimensional inputs, ranking (LambdaRank) or quantile objectives — anywhere training speed and memory are the bottleneck.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-47a1c97", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Level-wise vs leaf-wise tree growth?", "a": "<p>Level-wise (XGBoost default) splits all nodes at a depth before going deeper — balanced trees, more robust. Leaf-wise (LightGBM) always splits the leaf with the highest gain — reaches lower loss with fewer leaves, faster, but can produce deep unbalanced trees that overfit small data; constrain with num_leaves, min_data_in_leaf and max_depth.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-4e70f62", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are GOSS and EFB in LightGBM?", "a": "<p>GOSS (Gradient-based One-Side Sampling) keeps all high-gradient (under-fit) rows and a random sample of low-gradient rows, reweighting the sampled ones to keep gain estimates unbiased. EFB (Exclusive Feature Bundling) merges sparse features that are rarely non-zero simultaneously into one, reducing the number of features scanned. Both cut training cost with little accuracy loss.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-bcc9143", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What problem does CatBoost solve that other boosters don't?", "a": "<p>Categorical features without manual encoding and without target leakage. Its ordered target statistics encode each row's category using only earlier rows' labels, and ordered boosting computes residuals with models that haven't seen the row.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-e6c6070", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is target leakage in target encoding, and how does CatBoost avoid it?", "a": "<p>Naive target encoding replaces a category by its mean target computed on all rows, including the row itself, so the feature contains the answer (extreme for rare categories). The model looks great in training and fails on new data. CatBoost uses a random permutation and only rows before the current one, plus a prior for smoothing, so a row never sees its own label. K-fold target encoding is the manual alternative.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-a3f8566", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are oblivious (symmetric) trees and why use them?", "a": "<p>Trees where every node at a given depth uses the same split. A depth-d tree is d binary tests mapping to 2^d leaves — effectively a lookup table. They're strongly regularised (less overfitting), stable, and extremely fast to evaluate, which suits low-latency serving.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-390edae", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is prediction shift and how does ordered boosting fix it?", "a": "<p>In standard boosting, the residual for a row is computed by a model that was trained on that same row, so residuals on training data are systematically smaller than on new data — the gradient estimates are biased. Ordered boosting keeps models trained on prefixes of a permutation and computes each row's residual with a model that never saw it.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-1e74f40", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does CatBoost handle categorical features?", "a": "<p>With ordered target statistics: under a random permutation, each row's category is encoded with the (prior-smoothed) mean target of rows that come before it, so its own label doesn't leak. It also builds combinations of categorical features greedily. Ordered boosting applies the same idea to residual computation to avoid prediction shift.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-76218a0", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you pick CatBoost over LightGBM?", "a": "<p>Many (high-cardinality) categorical features, small-to-medium datasets where overfitting is a risk, limited tuning time (strong defaults), or when fast and stable inference matters (symmetric trees). LightGBM for very large datasets and fastest training.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-b3fa619", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Explain the K-means algorithm.", "a": "<p>Initialise K centroids; assign each point to the nearest centroid; recompute each centroid as the mean of its points; repeat until assignments stop changing. It minimises the within-cluster sum of squared distances (inertia).</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-eefacf4", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Does K-means always converge? To the global optimum?", "a": "<p>It always converges in a finite number of steps because both steps monotonically decrease (never increase) the objective and there are finitely many partitions. But only to a local minimum — results depend on initialisation, hence k-means++ and multiple restarts.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-a7e414a", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "How do you choose K?", "a": "<p>Elbow method on inertia, average silhouette score, Davies–Bouldin / Calinski–Harabasz indices, gap statistic, stability across bootstrap samples, and — often decisive — business interpretability/actionability of the segments.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-6be6522", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is k-means++ and why is it better?", "a": "<p>An initialisation that picks the first centroid randomly and each subsequent one with probability ∝ squared distance to the nearest chosen centroid. It spreads centroids out, avoids several centroids in one cluster, gives an O(log K) approximation guarantee in expectation, and speeds convergence.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-f471207", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What are the assumptions/limitations of K-means?", "a": "<p>Clusters are spherical, convex, similar in size and density; Euclidean geometry; features on the same scale; K known; sensitive to outliers and initialisation; numeric data only; every point assigned (no noise). Fails on moons/rings, elongated clusters, varied densities.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-fe352d6", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Why scale features before K-means?", "a": "<p>Euclidean distances are dominated by large-range features, so clusters would be formed almost entirely along those. Standardise (or use domain-meaningful weights) so each feature contributes appropriately.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-e8594d5", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is the silhouette score?", "a": "<p>For each point: s = (b − a)/max(a, b), where a is its mean distance to points in its own cluster and b its mean distance to points of the nearest other cluster. Ranges −1 to 1: near 1 well clustered, ~0 on a boundary, negative probably misassigned. Average over points to compare K.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-cd87fa5", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How is K-means related to Gaussian Mixture Models?", "a": "<p>K-means is the limit of EM for a GMM with equal, spherical covariances (σ²I, σ → 0) and equal weights, with hard instead of soft assignments. GMMs generalise it: elliptical clusters, different sizes, soft probabilistic memberships.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-3624b85", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How would you cluster categorical data?", "a": "<p>K-modes (matching dissimilarity, modes as centres), K-prototypes for mixed data, hierarchical clustering with Gower distance, or embed categories (e.g. entity embeddings / MCA) and then cluster.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-e74673e", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How do you evaluate clustering without labels — and with labels?", "a": "<p>Without: internal metrics (silhouette, DB, CH), stability across resamples, and qualitative/business review of segment profiles. With ground truth: Adjusted Rand Index, Normalised/Adjusted Mutual Information, homogeneity/completeness/V-measure.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-03178c4", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What's the complexity of K-means and how do you scale it?", "a": "<p>O(n·K·d·iterations) time, O((n + K)·d) memory. Scale with MiniBatchKMeans, Elkan's algorithm (triangle inequality), PCA to reduce d, sampling for initialisation, or distributed implementations (Spark MLlib).</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-2930d9b", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Give a business use case and how you'd validate the result.", "a": "<p>Customer segmentation on RFM (recency, frequency, monetary) features: log-transform and scale, choose K with silhouette + marketing input, profile clusters (means, sizes), name them (“lapsed high-value”), check stability over time, and validate by running targeted campaigns (A/B tests) per segment.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-55187eb", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "How does hierarchical clustering work and what's a dendrogram?", "a": "<p>Agglomerative: start with each point as a cluster and repeatedly merge the two closest clusters per the linkage criterion until one remains. The dendrogram is the tree of merges with heights = merge distances; cutting it horizontally gives a flat clustering, and long vertical gaps suggest natural cluster counts.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-e8de2d2", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Compare single, complete, average and Ward linkage.", "a": "<p>Single: min pairwise distance — follows chains, finds non-convex shapes but sensitive to noise. Complete: max distance — compact clusters, outlier-sensitive. Average: mean distance — balanced. Ward: minimises the increase in within-cluster variance — compact, equal-size clusters similar to K-means; requires Euclidean distance.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d948627", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Explain DBSCAN and its parameters.", "a": "<p>Density-based clustering: a core point has at least min_samples points within distance eps; clusters grow by connecting core points that are within eps of each other plus their border points; everything else is noise. eps sets the neighbourhood size, min_samples the density threshold. Choose eps from the knee of a k-distance plot.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-b38e992", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "When does DBSCAN fail?", "a": "<p>Clusters with very different densities (one eps can't fit all — use HDBSCAN or OPTICS), high-dimensional data where distances concentrate, unscaled features, and when eps is poorly chosen (too small → everything noise, too large → one cluster).</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-a5eb515", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "K-means vs GMM?", "a": "<p>K-means: hard assignments, spherical equal-variance clusters, minimises inertia, faster. GMM: soft probabilistic assignments, elliptical clusters with their own covariances and weights, maximises likelihood via EM, gives densities and supports BIC model selection. K-means is a limiting case of GMM.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d18ccf0", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Explain the EM algorithm.", "a": "<p>For models with latent variables: the E-step computes the posterior over latent variables (responsibilities) given current parameters; the M-step maximises the expected complete-data log-likelihood to update parameters. Repeat until convergence. Log-likelihood never decreases; converges to a local optimum, so use multiple inits.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-aab532e", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How does Isolation Forest detect anomalies?", "a": "<p>It builds random trees by picking a random feature and a random split value between its min and max. Anomalies are few and different, so they get isolated near the root — short average path lengths across trees. The anomaly score normalises the average path length by that expected for a random point (c(n)).</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-9e53f74", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is Local Outlier Factor?", "a": "<p>LOF compares a point's local reachability density to that of its k nearest neighbours. Points in a region much sparser than their neighbours' regions get LOF ≫ 1. It finds local outliers in datasets with clusters of different densities, unlike global distance rules.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-615f8d1", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How would you build an anomaly detection system for transactions without labels?", "a": "<p>Engineer behavioural features (amount vs user's history, velocity, new device/merchant, time-of-day), scale, fit an unsupervised detector (Isolation Forest / LOF / autoencoder), set the threshold by alert capacity (top-k per day), have analysts review alerts, and use their feedback as labels to move to a supervised model (with the unsupervised score as a feature). Monitor drift and alert precision.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d19251d", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How do you choose the number of components in a GMM?", "a": "<p>Fit over a range of K (and covariance types) and pick the lowest BIC = −2 log L + p log n (AIC uses 2p, tends to choose more components). Also check stability, cluster sizes and interpretability; a Bayesian GMM with a Dirichlet process prior can shrink unused components automatically.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-fa7f682", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between outlier detection and novelty detection?", "a": "<p>Outlier detection: training data contain outliers; find them in that data (unsupervised). Novelty detection: train on clean normal data only, then flag new observations that differ (semi-supervised), e.g. One-Class SVM or LOF(novelty=True).</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-3412d52", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "What is PCA and how does it work?", "a": "<p>An unsupervised linear transformation to new orthogonal axes (principal components) ordered by the variance they capture. Centre/standardise the data, compute the covariance matrix (or SVD), take eigenvectors sorted by eigenvalue, and project onto the top k to reduce dimensions while retaining most variance.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-171ccbf", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Why do we standardise data before PCA?", "a": "<p>PCA maximises variance, which depends on units. A feature measured in large units dominates PC1 regardless of its information content. Standardising gives every feature unit variance (PCA on the correlation matrix). Skip only when features share units and their raw variance matters.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-db89f03", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What do eigenvalues and eigenvectors represent in PCA?", "a": "<p>Eigenvectors of the covariance matrix are the principal directions (loadings); eigenvalues are the variances of the data along those directions. Eigenvalue / sum of eigenvalues = explained variance ratio.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-0a121f2", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How is PCA related to SVD?", "a": "<p>For centred X = USVᵀ, the columns of V are the principal directions, eigenvalues λ = s²/(n−1), and the PC scores are US. Libraries use SVD because it's more numerically stable than forming XᵀX, and randomized SVD scales to big data.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-2f2f740", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the number of principal components?", "a": "<p>Cumulative explained variance threshold (e.g. 95%), scree-plot elbow, Kaiser criterion, or — for predictive tasks — cross-validate n_components inside a pipeline with the downstream model.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-bad5238", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Are principal components correlated? Interpretable?", "a": "<p>Scores of different PCs are uncorrelated (orthogonal directions, diagonal covariance). Interpretation is hard because each PC mixes all original features; inspect loadings, or use sparse PCA / factor analysis with rotation for interpretability.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-aae924c", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Can PCA hurt model performance?", "a": "<p>Yes. PCA ignores the target, so it may discard low-variance directions that are highly predictive; it also loses interpretability. Tree models rarely benefit. Validate with CV rather than assuming.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-ea588e4", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "PCA vs LDA?", "a": "<p>PCA: unsupervised, maximises total variance, up to d components. LDA: supervised, maximises between-class vs within-class variance ratio, up to C − 1 components, assumes Gaussian classes with shared covariance. Use LDA when the goal is class separation.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-3cdd80e", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "t-SNE vs PCA — when do you use each?", "a": "<p>PCA: linear, deterministic, preserves global variance, fast, can transform new data — preprocessing and compression. t-SNE: non-linear, stochastic, preserves local neighbourhoods, slow, no out-of-sample transform — 2-D visualisation only. Don't read distances between t-SNE clusters or their sizes.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-0d8d911", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "What is the perplexity in t-SNE?", "a": "<p>A smooth measure of the effective number of neighbours each point considers (2^entropy of its neighbour distribution). Low perplexity emphasises very local structure (many small clumps); high perplexity considers broader neighbourhoods. Typical 5–50; try several values.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-c5b479a", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How is PCA related to linear autoencoders?", "a": "<p>A linear autoencoder with a k-unit bottleneck trained on squared reconstruction error learns a subspace equal to the span of the top-k principal components (though not necessarily the orthogonal PC basis). Non-linear autoencoders generalise PCA.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-36b6c47", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Does PCA help with multicollinearity in regression?", "a": "<p>Yes — principal component regression uses uncorrelated PCs as predictors, removing collinearity, at the cost of interpretability and possibly dropping predictive low-variance directions. Ridge regression is a soft version that shrinks low-variance directions most.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-6ccaaca", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 1, "q": "What is stationarity and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>A weakly stationary series has constant mean and variance over time and autocovariance that depends only on lag. Models like ARMA assume it so that patterns learned from the past apply to the future; non-stationary series can produce spurious correlations and unreliable forecasts. Check with plots, ADF and KPSS; fix with differencing or transforms.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-f02a434", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose p, d, q for ARIMA?", "a": "<p>d: number of differences needed for stationarity (ADF/KPSS). p: lag where the PACF cuts off (AR order). q: lag where the ACF cuts off (MA order). Refine by AIC/BIC grid search (auto_arima) and verify residuals are white noise (Ljung–Box).</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-fb71d12", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 1, "q": "Why can't you use regular K-fold cross-validation for time series?", "a": "<p>Shuffling puts future observations in training and past ones in validation, leaking future information and producing over-optimistic scores; autocorrelated neighbours also leak. Use time-ordered splits (expanding/sliding window, TimeSeriesSplit), with a gap for look-back features.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-058a053", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "ACF vs PACF?", "a": "<p>ACF at lag k is the correlation between y_t and y_{t−k}, including indirect effects through intermediate lags. PACF at lag k is the correlation after controlling for lags 1…k−1 — the direct effect. AR(p) shows PACF cut-off at p; MA(q) shows ACF cut-off at q.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-3788dda", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How would you forecast sales for 10,000 products?", "a": "<p>A global model (LightGBM) trained across all series with lag/rolling/calendar/price/promo features and product/store categoricals, predicting scaled or differenced targets; hierarchical reconciliation if forecasts must sum across levels; special handling for intermittent and new (cold-start) products; rolling-origin backtests; WAPE/MASE metrics; baselines per series.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-4e434d0", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "What are the pitfalls of MAPE?", "a": "<p>Undefined/infinite when actuals are zero, explodes for small actuals, asymmetric (over-forecasts can be penalised without bound while under-forecasts cap at 100%), and it favours models that under-forecast. Alternatives: WAPE, sMAPE, MASE.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-b46e92f", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "Recursive vs direct multi-step forecasting?", "a": "<p>Recursive: one 1-step model, feed predictions back as inputs — simple but errors compound. Direct: separate model per horizon — no compounding but more models and ignores dependencies between horizons. Hybrids and multi-output models sit in between.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-f5b95c3", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do tree models handle trend in time series?", "a": "<p>Poorly — they can't extrapolate beyond the training target range. Remove trend first (differencing, ratio to a rolling mean, detrending with a linear model) and let the trees model the stationary remainder, or combine a linear trend component with tree residual models.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-db50571", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 3, "q": "What is a unit root?", "a": "<p>In y_t = ρ y_{t−1} + ε_t, ρ = 1 is a unit root: a random walk whose shocks persist forever and variance grows with time — non-stationary. ADF tests H₀: unit root. First differencing turns a random walk into stationary white noise.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-386fa8c", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle multiple seasonalities?", "a": "<p>Fourier terms for each period as regressors (e.g. daily + weekly + yearly), TBATS, Prophet (multiple seasonal components), MSTL decomposition, or calendar features in ML models.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-38b2fd7", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you produce prediction intervals?", "a": "<p>From model-based standard errors (ARIMA/ETS), quantile regression (LightGBM quantile objective / pinball loss), bootstrapped residual simulation, or conformal prediction with time-aware calibration. Evaluate with coverage and interval width.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-396a10f", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 1, "q": "What is MLOps and why is it needed?", "a": "<p>The set of practices to reliably build, deploy, monitor and improve ML systems in production — combining ML, data engineering and DevOps. It's needed because models depend on changing data, fail silently, must be reproducible and auditable, and need retraining; without automation, models rot and releases are slow and risky.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-4551e9b", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between data drift and concept drift?", "a": "<p>Data (covariate) drift: the distribution of inputs P(X) changes (e.g. a new customer demographic). Concept drift: the relationship between inputs and target P(Y|X) changes (e.g. fraudsters adapt, so the same features now mean something else). Data drift is detectable without labels; concept drift usually needs labels or proxy signals.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-8babe50", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you detect drift in production?", "a": "<p>Compare production windows against a reference (training or a stable period): per-feature statistics and tests (PSI, KS, χ², Jensen–Shannon/Wasserstein), prediction-distribution monitoring, multivariate detectors (domain classifier: train a model to distinguish reference vs production — AUC ≫ 0.5 means drift), data-quality checks, and performance metrics once labels arrive. Alert on effect-size thresholds, not just p-values.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-229cf22", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What should you monitor for a deployed model?", "a": "<p>Service health (latency percentiles, errors, throughput, resources), data quality (schema, nulls, ranges, new categories, freshness, volume), input and prediction drift, model performance on labelled feedback (overall and by segment), business KPIs, fairness metrics, and cost.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-064b4cf", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What is training–serving skew and how do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>A mismatch between the data/features the model saw in training and what it receives in production — different code paths, data sources, timing or preprocessing. Prevent with a feature store (shared definitions, point-in-time correct offline data), packaging preprocessing inside the model artifact, logging serving features and training on them, and skew monitors comparing online vs offline features.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-92cf9c6", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Batch vs real-time inference — how do you choose?", "a": "<p>Batch when predictions can be precomputed and a delay is acceptable (daily churn scores) — cheaper and simpler. Real-time when the decision depends on fresh context at request time (fraud at checkout, search ranking) — needs low-latency serving, online features and higher operational cost. Streaming in between for continuous event-driven scoring.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-e1cf367", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Explain shadow, canary and blue–green deployments.", "a": "<p>Shadow: new model gets a copy of live traffic but its predictions aren't used — safe validation. Canary: a small share of real traffic goes to the new model, ramped up if metrics hold. Blue–green: two full environments; traffic switches from blue (old) to green (new) at once, enabling instant rollback.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-aad0311", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What is a feature store?", "a": "<p>A centralised system for defining, computing, storing and serving features: an offline store (historical values with point-in-time joins for training) and an online store (low-latency latest values for inference), with metadata, sharing/reuse across teams, lineage and monitoring. Examples: Feast, Tecton, Databricks, Vertex AI, SageMaker Feature Store.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-c9160cc", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What does a model registry provide?", "a": "<p>Versioned storage of model artifacts with metadata (metrics, parameters, training data and code versions), lifecycle stages/aliases (staging, production, archived), approval workflows, lineage, and a single source of truth that deployment pipelines pull from — enabling reproducibility, audits and rollbacks.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-557f857", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you decide when to retrain a model?", "a": "<p>Based on how fast the domain changes and the cost of staleness vs retraining: scheduled retraining matched to the drift rate, triggered retraining on drift/performance alerts, or online learning for fast-moving data. Use backtests to estimate how performance decays with model age, and always gate new models with validation and canaries.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-d749a7d", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you ensure reproducibility of ML experiments?", "a": "<p>Version code (Git), data (snapshots/DVC/hashes) and environment (pinned dependencies, Docker); fix random seeds; log parameters, metrics and artifacts with experiment tracking; parameterise pipelines with configs; record lineage in the model registry.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-1682bd6", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "Your model's accuracy dropped suddenly in production. Walk through your investigation.", "a": "<p>Verify the alert (label delay, metric bug). Find the start time and correlate with deployments and upstream changes. Check data quality (schema, nulls, units, broken joins, stale features). Measure feature and prediction drift and per-segment performance. Compare online vs offline features for skew. Mitigate (rollback/fallback/fix pipeline), retrain if concept drift, then add monitors/tests and write a post-mortem.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-ad41255", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What tests would you put in an ML CI pipeline?", "a": "<p>Unit tests for feature transformations; data validation (schema, ranges, null rates, distribution expectations); training smoke test on a small sample; model quality gates vs the current champion on a fixed holdout and critical slices; invariance/directional tests (e.g. increasing income shouldn't increase default risk); latency and artifact-size checks; fairness checks; API contract tests.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-8797829", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you handle delayed ground-truth labels when monitoring?", "a": "<p>Monitor leading indicators: input drift, prediction-distribution drift, data quality, business proxies (e.g. early repayment signals). Estimate performance with calibrated confidence (CBPE). Build pipelines that join labels when they arrive and backfill performance dashboards; set retraining cadence around label availability.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-f3cef22", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "What are feedback loops in ML systems and how do you mitigate them?", "a": "<p>When predictions influence the data the model is later trained on — recommendations shape clicks; credit models only observe outcomes for approved applicants; predictive policing. They bias training data and can amplify errors. Mitigate with exploration (randomised holdouts, bandits), logging propensities and using inverse-propensity weighting, reject inference, and monitoring diversity/coverage.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-7de8cd0", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you serve a model with very tight latency (e.g. &lt; 10 ms)?", "a": "<p>Precompute heavy features and cache them in an online store; use a lightweight model or distil/quantise a big one; export to an optimised runtime (ONNX Runtime, Treelite for trees); keep the model in memory in a compiled service; batch where possible; colocate with callers; autoscale; set timeouts with a fallback (default score or rules).</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-3cc2946", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you design a system to detect fake reviews?", "a": "<p>Clarify the action (hide, downrank, review) and costs. Labels from moderation decisions and known fraud rings (noisy; use active learning). Features: text (embeddings, repetitiveness, sentiment extremity), reviewer behaviour (account age, review velocity, rating variance, burstiness on one product), graph signals (reviewer–product bipartite communities), metadata (IP/device sharing, verified purchase). Model: GBM on behavioural + text features, graph-based detection for rings. Metrics: precision at moderation capacity, recall on known rings; monitor adversarial shifts; human review loop.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-2a65492", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you define the label for a churn model?", "a": "<p>Work with the business: explicit cancellation for subscriptions; for non-contractual businesses, inactivity beyond a threshold chosen from the return-probability curve (e.g. 95% of users inactive for 45 days never return). Fix a prediction point and horizon (churn within 30 days after snapshot), exclude already-churned users, and keep feature windows strictly before the snapshot.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-3cae099", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you handle the cold-start problem in recommendations?", "a": "<p>New users: popular/trending items, onboarding questions, contextual signals (location, device, referrer), session-based recommendations that adapt within the first clicks. New items: content-based features/embeddings (text, image, category, price), exploration boosts or bandits to gather interactions, similar-item mapping.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-7f16aa5", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "How would you evaluate a recommender offline and online?", "a": "<p>Offline: time-split holdout of interactions; recall@k for candidate generation; NDCG@k/MAP/MRR for ranking; coverage, diversity, novelty; counterfactual/off-policy evaluation with logged propensities (IPS) to correct exposure bias. Online: A/B test on CTR, conversion, revenue/session, retention, with guardrails (latency, diversity, complaints).</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-29df504", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Batch-scored model or real-time API for a credit-limit increase campaign?", "a": "<p>Batch: eligibility and offers are decided periodically, latency isn't critical, features come from monthly statements, and batch is cheaper, easier to audit and explain. Real-time only if the offer is triggered by in-session events.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-7588906", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you predict ETA for a food delivery app?", "a": "<p>Decompose into stages (restaurant prep time, courier assignment & pickup, travel time) with separate models or a model with stage features; features: restaurant historical prep times by hour, current order load, courier location & availability, route distance, real-time traffic, weather; GBM with quantile loss for P50/P90; evaluate MAE and on-time %; online calibration; monitor during peaks and adjust quickly with real-time features.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-215d807", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "How would you ensure fairness in a loan approval model?", "a": "<p>Exclude protected attributes as inputs but test for proxies; measure fairness metrics across groups (approval-rate parity, equal opportunity/TPR parity, calibration within groups); analyse trade-offs with business and legal; mitigations: reweighting, constraints during training, group-specific thresholds where legal; explainability (reason codes, adverse-action notices); documentation (model cards), monitoring and periodic audits.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-34699f0", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Your model will be used to rank search results. Which loss and metrics?", "a": "<p>Learning-to-rank: pointwise (regression/classification of relevance), pairwise (RankNet, LambdaRank) or listwise (LambdaMART, ListNet). LambdaMART (LightGBM/XGBoost rank:ndcg) is a strong default. Offline NDCG@10/MRR from graded relevance labels or click models (correct position bias); online CTR, successful sessions, time to click, abandonment.</p>"}];
